# modules/dast/passive/checks.rules - the §7.1 passive script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md tier 2).
#
# checks_registry_load (lib/checks.sh) globs every *.rules under modules/dast/
# with no per-file allowlist, so this file and modules/dast/active/checks.rules
# load together on any `scan.sh dast` run; the linter enforces one owning module
# (DAST) for every record in both.
#
# This file currently registers DAST-07's transport-security checks only.  The
# other tier-2 passive tickets (DAST-05, 06, 08, 09, 10, 11) are peers with no
# ordering between them and were run in parallel with this one, so each adds its
# own records here rather than any of them owning the file.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `passive`, MATCHING THE PHASE TABLE'S `passive/tls.sh:passive`
# FLOOR (modules/dast/engine.sh).  Both gates must permit and neither can widen
# the other - the intersection rule docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 15 froze.  These
# checks are genuinely passive: one TLS handshake per target is opened, no
# application data is sent, no request is made, and nothing on the target
# changes.  None of them carries `intrusive`.
#
# `quick` and `compliance` are on every record here.  `quick` because the whole
# family costs ONE handshake for the target regardless of how many of them fire
# - the cheapest real coverage a dast run can buy - and `compliance` because
# certificate validity and protocol/cipher strength are the transport controls
# every framework in docs/DESIGN.md §8.7's list enumerates.
#
# ONE CHECK ID PER PROPERTY, not one per phase.  The DAST fingerprint
# (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 5) is (target, method, path_template,
# param_location, param_name) plus the check id; a TLS finding's location is the
# listener rather than a request, so every finding this phase emits for one
# endpoint shares all five location components.  The check id is therefore the
# ONLY thing that keeps an expired certificate and a weak protocol on the same
# listener from colliding into one finding, which is why "expired" and
# "expiring soon" are two ids rather than one id with two severities.

id: DAST-TLS-WEAK_PROTOCOL-01
title: TLS endpoint negotiated a deprecated protocol version
script: passive/tls.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-327
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Disable SSLv2, SSLv3, TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 on this listener and
  offer TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 only (RFC 8996). Where a client genuinely cannot be
  upgraded, terminate its traffic on a separate listener with its own hostname
  so the deprecated version is not offered to every client of this one.
references: CWE-327
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
references: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8996

id: DAST-TLS-WEAK_CIPHER-01
title: TLS endpoint negotiated a weak cipher suite
script: passive/tls.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-327
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Restrict the listener cipher list to AEAD suites with forward
  secrecy (ECDHE or DHE key exchange with AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305). Remove
  every NULL, anonymous, export, RC4, single-DES, 3DES, IDEA, SEED and MD5
  suite. On TLS 1.3 leave the default suite set alone and fix the TLS 1.2 list
  instead.
references: CWE-327
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TLS-CERT_EXPIRED-01
title: TLS certificate has expired
script: passive/tls.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-324
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Renew the certificate and reload the listener. Automate renewal
  (ACME or the platform certificate manager) and alert on the remaining
  lifetime, so the next renewal is not a manual step that can be missed again.
references: CWE-324
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TLS-CERT_EXPIRING-01
title: TLS certificate expires soon
script: passive/tls.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-324
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Renew the certificate before it expires and confirm the listener
  picks up the new one. If renewal is already automated, check that the
  automation is running and that its own alerting fires - a silent renewal
  failure looks exactly like a working one until the day it expires. The
  warning window is config/scanner.conf's tls-expiry-warn-days, default 30.
references: CWE-324
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TLS-SELF_SIGNED-01
title: TLS certificate is self-signed and chains to no trusted issuer
script: passive/tls.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-295
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Replace the certificate with one issued by a certificate authority
  the intended clients already trust, and serve the full intermediate chain. If
  this endpoint is internal and must use a private CA, distribute that CA to the
  clients and keep them verifying - never disable verification in the client,
  which is the change this finding usually provokes and is strictly worse than
  the certificate it works around.
references: CWE-295
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TLS-WILDCARD_CERT-01
title: TLS endpoint serves a wildcard certificate where a host-specific one is expected
script: passive/tls.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
requires-cmd: openssl
remediation: Issue a certificate whose subjectAltName names this host
  specifically, so a compromise of its private key cannot impersonate every
  sibling host under the wildcard label. If a wildcard is genuinely the intended
  design for this target, record that by setting tls-expect-wildcard true on its
  config/scope.conf entry, which makes the expectation explicit and reviewable
  rather than making this finding disappear silently.
references: CWE-693
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
